{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<system-reminder>\n<tools-used-before-summary>Bash, Read</tools-used-before-summary>\n</system-reminder>\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The only user request was the `/goal` command: **\"Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.\"** A session-scoped Stop hook enforces that condition.\n   - STAGE.md: \"Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\"\n   - TASK.md: an original game inspired by *Animal Crossing: City Folk*, on the riftlight Pixel Engine kit (engine unmodified), single-HTML deliverable `[GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-171818].html` beside a same-named dev subfolder, music at 10% default, gibberish voices in code, AI Hub multiplayer plus standalone multiplayer, dev mode with live sliders and reset, attract mode at 10 s, 10-foot controller-first UI, touch-only notice, no TODOs or stubs.\n   - **CRITICAL hand-in:** \"Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen.\" Plus `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML.\n   - Standing constraints from TASK.md (still apply): \"Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine's ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks ... Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\" \"Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\" Keep one or two browsers open; keep all files in own folder, not /tmp or parent; don't run `playwright install`.\n   - Harness rules: commit or push only when the user asks (no commit was requested). Report outcomes faithfully. Use they/them for anyone whose pronouns aren't stated.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Riftlight Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (WebGPU, three.js r186 as `THREE`, Rapier idle), 480×270 pixel-art pipeline, `aspect:'fixed'`; browser flags `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader`.\n   - Single HTML: session.js first script, engine byte-for-byte in a `text/plain` script turned into an importmap blob, packs as data URLs in `window.__PIXEL_ASSETS__`, esbuild bundle inline with `--external:./pixel-engine.js`.\n   - One `HonkGame` object, one plain-JSON `world` as the save; places (town at origin, City at x=500, rooms at x=1000, bus at x=1500) with local coordinates and actors parented to `place.group`; own tile collision and `NavGrid`.\n   - Generator-based scenes (`Scripts.run`, `yield`, `yield seconds`, `yield*`); UI screen stack with `Menu`, `Dialogue`, focus and hit rects; `GameInput` with named actions and last-device tracking.\n   - Models, textures, clips and songs as data: `model()`, `textureDef`, `compileClips` on a 7-joint rig, `Song` with `stepsPerBeat:3`, `SoundDef` formant voices.\n   - AI Hub Session Protocol v1 shared-world; Visit with BroadcastChannel and serverless WebRTC (`CompressionStream('deflate-raw')` invite codes).\n   - Testing with `Engine.headless` in Node at about 0.4 ms a frame, Playwright (Python) on `file://`, stub `navigator.getGamepads`, touch profiles, harness over `python3 -u -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections (dev folder = `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-171818__x10629c/work/HonkIfYouOwe-AnimalCrossingCityFolk-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-171818`, the cwd; its parent `work/` holds PLAN.md, TASK.md, STAGE.md, PROGRESS.md, the HTML and exit-interview.json):\n   - **Deliverables:** `../HonkIfYouOwe-AnimalCrossingCityFolk-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-171818.html` (8.0 MB, built 19:24), `../exit-interview.json` (written by `tools/exit_interview.mjs` from `src/data/about.js`; parses; keys sourceInformation, testingFeedback, engineFeedback, promptFeedback, aiHub), `../PROGRESS.md`.\n   - **Entry and core:** `src/main.js`, `src/game.js` (HonkGame; `handle()` exposes `window.__GAME__` with `world, ui.model(), tuning, status(), fingerprint(), engine, visit, devLineup, actions{newGame, continueGame, quickStart, goto, setDay}`), `src/engine.js`, `src/pixel-engine.js` (identical to `kit/pixel-engine.js`, confirmed with `cmp`), `src/gameDefaults.js`.\n   - `src/core/`: storage, rng (`Stream`, `dayStream`), events, calendar (`villageDay`, `realDay`, `calendar`), weather, save (`newWorld`, migrations, `SAVE_VERSION=2`).\n   - `src/world/`: tiles, terrainMesh, town (`TownPlace`), objectsView, startObjects, place (`moveWithCollision`), occluders (dissolve 0.72, rim = own colour), rooms (`RoomPlace`), home (`HomeRoom`), roomPlaces, city (`CityPlace`).\n   - `src/actors/`: rig (`Actor`, `setUmbrella`, spring tail), clips (48), player, npc.\n   - `src/systems/`: script, time, sky, interact (`Interact`, `worldInput`), townVerbs, tools, reveal, actions (`ACTIONS`, `runAction`), services, venues, bus (`BusPlace`, `busRide`, `ballad`), stargaze, flow (`newDay`, `dailyRespawn`, `handleAbsence`, `onHourChange`), life (`Life`), economy, memory, lines, say, talk, favours, mail, cheese, rating, townRating, patterns, pocketActions, shop, dayone, story, newgame, session, bell, festivals/festivals.\n   - `src/ui/`: font, input, draw, glyphs, ui, dialogue; `screens/`: title, pause, settings, about, dev, pockets, map, almanac, emote, mail, letter, keyboard, numbers, catalogue, chime, pattern, placement, bed, clockFace, lookEditor, compare, auction, theatre, credits.\n   - `src/audio/`: sfx, voice, composer (101 songs), music, ambience.\n   - `src/data/`: town, townMap, cityMap, rooms, items (284), fish, bugs, fossils, art, furniture, clothes, neighbours, names, year, about, dialogue/{types,unique,hosts}.\n   - `src/art/`: critter, models, itemModels, textures, paintings, paintingSprites, icons, build (`buildAny`), sheets.\n   - `src/net/`: transport, protocol, arena (`BazaarGame`), hubStart, visit, visitUi. `src/dev/`: tuning, attract, fingerprint, overlay.\n   - **Tests:** `tests/run.mjs` (builds first for page suites), `tests/sim/{sim,cli}.mjs`, `tests/play/{boot,bot,playthrough,part2,part3,replay,monkey,cli}.mjs`, `tests/browser/browser_test.py`, `tests/hub/{run_hub.py,harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}`, `tests/visit/visit_test.py`.\n   - **Tools:** `tools/build.mjs`, `genmap.mjs`, `modelcheck.mjs`, `clipcheck.mjs`, `songcheck.mjs`, `smoke.sh`, `shot.py` (screenshot helper writing to `shots/`), `exit_interview.mjs`. Also `package.json` (`\"type\":\"module\"`), `node_modules` (playwright 1.63.0), `kit/`, `vendor/session.js`, `vendor/HUB-README.md`.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - Boot and data crashes: waterfall param out of range; 52 model problems on first validation (undeclared slots, below floor); texture `dir:'v'`→`'x'`; clip layer period mismatch; `buildModel` throwing on over-specified furniture palettes (added `src/art/build.js` `buildAny`); fish `len` param below minimum in the Aquarium (clamped); invalid painting SVG path crash in the Gallery.\n   - Gameplay bugs: Confirm press that closed a menu also fired a world action (added `wasBlocked` guard); Seed Toss froze the player (`Scripts.setFree`); player stuck after being seated (depenetration in `moveWithCollision`); furniture could trap you at the door (wider clearance, `unstickPlayer`); Bartleby asleep during his Day One errand (wake 7); unread mail labels never updated; tree reach too short; rooms lacked pathfinding (`syncNav`); Hibernate jumped to the wrong day; attract loop too short (`BEAT_STRETCH = 2.1`); font missing `à`.\n   - Browser-only: key taps shorter than a frame lost in menu nav; tap didn't exit attract on touch (window `pointerdown` → `input.send({t:'tap'})`).\n   - Visual: wallpaper z-fighting on west wall, dim rooms too dark, white occluder noise, tool ring on load, soup hidden under the rim, Mrs. Gander at the origin in the opening (actorOverride now updated each frame), night bus unreadable (interior light), loud floors calmed.\n   - Review-found: Rind and Vesper only placed at day start (now hourly), right-stick flick repeated every frame (edge-detected), Day One listeners lost on reload (`listenForErrands` at boot), night bus autosaving (skipped while `story.stage==='new'`), engine blocking Backspace in the Gate Pass box.\n   - Tooling: `npm init` created `\"type\":\"commonjs\"` and broke ESM imports (fixed package.json). Slow first software-GPU frames made 120 ms pad presses miss (tests now hold across real frames). One failure in the second-to-last full run was a new Visit test against an older build; it passed after rebuild.\n   - No user feedback on any of these.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Final full run `node tests/run.mjs`: **2272 checks, 0 failures, exit 0** on the final build (sim 2013, play 124 milestones with replay, attract loop and monkey; browser; Hub all 4 scenarios; Visit 18; sheets exit 0). No source has changed since.\n   - An unrecognised process `python3 tests/browser/visit_suite.py` (PID 3007376) turned out to belong to a sibling agent (`claude-opus-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-171821__x10629c`, same suffix); left alone. Check against my exact folder found only my own shell, so no stray processes of mine.\n   - Mirror and TV check in the real browser: `{\"place\":\"home\",\"mirrors\":[{\"renders\":3,\"enabled\":true}],\"tv\":{\"renders\":1,\"enabled\":true},\"townVisible\":true,\"stops\":1,\"tvStop\":true}`, no engine or GPU errors. Both really render. The close-up screenshot `shots/mirror_tv.png` was taken but not yet looked at.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - `/goal` \"Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.\"\n   - No other user messages. Task-completion notifications and \"say what you're doing\" nudges came from the system or harness, not the user, and no approval has been given by the user.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Optionally view `shots/mirror_tv.png` to confirm by eye that the mirror and TV look right. If wrong, fix, rebuild and rerun the affected suites.\n   - Remove `shots/` again (recreated for the check).\n   - Send the final chat reply with Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback (required by TASK.md), plus honest caveats.\n\n8. Current Work:\n   All build and test work is finished; I was doing last-mile verification before the final reply. The last step ran `tools/shot.py` in the real browser, placed a TV and mirror at home, and read the counters above, proving both render. Then came this summary request.\n\n   Text for the final reply (verbatim from `src/data/about.js` and exit-interview.json):\n\n   **Source Information**\n   - Date: 2026-11-05 17:18:18\n   - Source: Animal Crossing: City Folk\n   - Plan: PLAN.md\n   - Genre: Life sim / social sim\n   - Perspective: Isometric 3/4 orthographic diorama with 90-degree camera turns; dollhouse cutaway indoors\n   - Tech: Riftlight Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (WebGPU, three.js r186 inside), plain JavaScript modules bundled by esbuild 0.28.2 into one HTML file; AI Hub session client 1.4.0; WebRTC and BroadcastChannel for Visit\n   - Art Direction: The engine's native 3D-as-pixel-art with the default look (Sweetie 16, toon bands, pixel outlines); new critter generator, buildings, trees, items and paintings as data; Kenney Furniture and Nature packs (CC0), recoloured\n   - Animation: The engine's clip format compiled with compileClips onto a seven-joint rig, played with THREE.AnimationMixer and crossFadeTo; code-driven blink, talk, squash, head turns; 48 clips\n   - Audio: All code. Music through the engine's Song sequencer (24 hourly arrangements with rain and snow variants built from 12 motifs, plus venues, festivals and records), default 10% volume. Sound effects as SoundDefs. Animalese-style gibberish voices from formant-shaped harmonics\n   - Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (shared-world round, The Bean Bazaar, 1-4 players); on its own, Visit: BroadcastChannel on one computer and serverless WebRTC with invite codes, up to 3 guests\n   - Testing: One command, node tests/run.mjs: rule checks in Node, a scripted playthrough of the whole game with replay, an attract loop and input monkey on Engine.headless, Playwright browser checks on file:// with stub pad and touch profiles, the AI Hub harness in all four scenarios, two-page Visit tests, and model sheets\n   - Research: None beyond the supplied plan, the engine kit docs and the Hub README\n   - Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n   - Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n   - Usage: added on import\n\n   **Testing Feedback**\n   - How many issues did testing catch? About 28 that needed a fix: seven crashes or bad data at build and load time, eleven gameplay bugs found by the scripted playthrough and the input monkey, two input bugs found only in the real browser, and eight things I only saw in screenshots.\n   - How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking? Eight. Entering the store, the Aquarium and the Gallery each crashed; a menu-closing press also fired a world action; the Seed Toss froze the player; furniture could trap you at the door; Bartleby slept through his own Day One errand; and Mrs. Gander was missing from the opening scene.\n   - What was the most helpful test you ran? The scripted playthrough on Engine.headless: real input, about 0.4 ms a frame, every milestone from the night bus to Year Two in under a minute. It found most of the gameplay bugs and let me rerun everything after each change.\n   - What was the least helpful test you ran? The 279 warnings from the model checker. A few were useful, but most were about coplanar faces and small floating parts that look fine at game scale, so I mostly stopped reading them and judged from screenshots and the cast line-up.\n\n   **Engine Feedback**\n   - What did you like most about the engine? Models and clips as plain data, plus Engine.headless. I could write a whole cast of 38 characters from one generator, and run the entire game in Node with the same code that runs on WebGPU.\n   - Did you get stuck or guess wrong? Several small guesses. buildModel throws if a palette names a slot the model lacks, which broke shared furniture palettes. Model params are hard range-checked. I guessed the renderSound signature wrong. The dissolve front is white unless a rim colour is set. And a press shorter than a frame shows in wasPressed but not isDown, which lost taps at low frame rates.\n   - What was the most important thing you had to build yourself? The whole UI layer: a mixed-case bitmap font, a screen stack with focus and hit areas, dialogue with voices, and prompts that follow the last device. Close behind were the generator-based scene runner and the tile world with its own collision.\n   - What was the hardest part about using the engine? Judging what a screen really looks like. Screenshots on the software GPU are slow, the first frames take seconds while pipelines compile, and Mirror and RenderView cannot be exercised headless, so visual bugs surfaced late compared with logic bugs.\n   - What did you change in the engine, and why? None. pixel-engine.js ships byte for byte, and a test asserts it.\n   - If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be? Mixed-case text in the HUD with a retained widget layer for focus and hit areas. Every game with menus has to rebuild that, and it was the largest single piece of work outside the game itself.\n\n   **Prompt Feedback**\n   - What did you like most about this prompt? The plan is concrete and honest about what was verified. The tested recipes, such as the browser flags and the importmap packaging, worked first time and saved hours. The writing samples also set a clear voice to match.\n   - What was confusing? Little. I had to decide how to write the source in the file name, since a colon cannot go in one, so I used AnimalCrossingCityFolk. The plan also asks for a stargazing camera glide that an orthographic ground view cannot show, so I used a full-screen star field instead.\n   - What was the hardest thing to do? Breadth at a consistent quality. Every system ships together, including eight venues, four festivals, three contests, a Hub round and two Visit transports, and each needs its own small joke. Keeping all of it wired together was harder than any single feature.\n   - What would you change in this prompt? I would name the exact file-name form for sources with punctuation, and say which plan details are firm and which are only suggestions. Content floors could also be lower for the cosmetic lists, so more effort goes to feel.\n\n   **Caveats to state honestly in the final reply:**\n   - Checked only with stand-ins: stub DualSense (no physical pad), Web Audio analyser levels (nobody listened), Playwright phone and tablet profiles, WebKit (not real iOS), and WebRTC on one machine with `iceServers: []` (real internet STUN untested).\n   - The playthrough drives real input but uses shortcuts: teleports, forced clock values, directly given items. The replay comparison covers a shorter, purely input-driven run. The Hub harness ran with `limit=20`, not a full five-minute round.\n   - Pacing, balance and the 2.5–5 hour length have not been played by a person.\n   - Known deviations: stargazing is a full-screen star field, not a camera glide with `Timeline`; constellations are marked and found, with fixed names, not named by the player. 279 model-checker warnings remain (sheets exit 0).\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Finish the wrap-up that was in progress: optionally look at `shots/mirror_tv.png`, delete `shots/` from the dev folder, confirm `../` still holds the HTML and `exit-interview.json`, then send the final reply. The last stated intent was: \"I never objectively confirmed the mirror and TV actually render, only that they raise no GPU errors. Before reporting them as working, I'll read their render counters in the real browser and look at them up close.\" The counters are done; only the close look and the final reply with the required sections remain. The final reply should say plainly what was built, the test result (2272 checks, 0 failures), where the files are, the caveats above, and the Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback text. Don't commit or push, since that wasn't requested.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build-v2--20261105-171818--x10629c-work/a4cb4937-9264-42cc-b44d-b76eca04b6e6.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened.\n\nThe messages after this summary are the most recent messages from before compaction, kept verbatim. The summary was written without seeing them, so something it says has not happened yet may already have happened in them."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4cb4937-9264-42cc-b44d-b76eca04b6e6","uuid":"46f